); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/9` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md-1072-\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1073:**Prontidão da review antes da selagem.** Uma nova selagem de escopo de spec recusa o que `pose close` e a preflight da conclusão só recusariam depois de uma rodada completa de review: uma mudança em raiz de entrega sem declaração de entrega (`undeclared-delivery`) ou em caminho governado que os artefatos não declaram (`undeclared`), lidas do mesmo índice que `pose close` lê, e, quando um critério obrigatório pede mapeamentos estruturais, uma decisão ou premissa que não alcança requisito nem restrição (dê a ela uma linha `- Basis:`). Um bundle já selado é devolvido como estava. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <arquivo|-> [--json]` relata todo motivo pelo qual a conclusão recusaria um rascunho (bloco ilegível, rodada não contabilizada, critério, mapeamento, achado, evidência ou ferramenta que a preflight da atestação recusa) e não grava nada; dentro de uma review delegada o run fornece o bundle e a rodada por `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` e nomeia o próprio motor em `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, e `-` lê o rascunho da entrada padrão, então funciona num sandbox somente leitura. O brief do template `review-brief/8` pede ao revisor que o rode antes de terminar; o `review-brief/9` também manda rodar cada comando `pose` do plano como `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, porque o `pose` que o shell do revisor resolve pode ser um motor mais antigo. `pose delegate` nomeia um arquivo de nota grande demais com seu tamanho e o limite, e um run concluído que nada concluiu automaticamente imprime o comando `pose review conclude <run>` que o registra.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md-1074-\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md-1075-**Requests de review delegada.** `pose review request <escopo|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <nome,...>] [--note-file <caminho>] [--apply]` abre uma action request de kind `delegated-review` que vincula o projeto, o worktree, o escopo, o bundle selado, o brief, as notas, as policies, os agentes aceitos e o destino da conclusão (uma atestação, um registro de adjudicação ou evidência de smoke). Nenhum ator a responde: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <chave> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <arquivo>] [--apply]`, ou a tool MCP `pose_action_fulfil`, submete um run concluído, e o motor registra o fulfillment só quando o run é do brief e do bundle do request, de um agente aceito, concluído com sua contenção, sua prova assinada verifica, seu ledger de tentativas está íntegro e o motor registrou sua conclusão no destino do request; o fulfillment é verificado de novo a cada leitura. `pose delegate review <escopo> --request <act-id>` roda o brief do request localmente e submete o run depois de concluído. Um run que rejeita cumpre o request sem aprovar nada; um bundle superado ou uma policy alterada o invalida, mesmo já cumprido, e ele é pedido de novo com `--supersedes <act-id>`, que o aposenta sob as regras de autoridade de supersession (`--authority <json>`, `--role <papel>`, `--requested-at` do preview) ou, quando o plano passa a reservar a review a uma pessoa, faz da escalação para a pessoa o seu sucessor; um retry com a mesma chave e o mesmo run não faz nada. Uma pessoa é chamada por exceção, como decision request ao papel maintainer: um plano que reserva a review a uma pessoa, um orçamento de tentativas esgotado, revisores em conflito e uma `business_decision` que um revisor levantou no draft. A prova de um run verifica em qualquer checkout do projeto: o worktree onde rodou fica registrado, não é exigido de quem lê, então um job de CI ou o Conductor cumpre o request com o mesmo envelope.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md-1076-\n--\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-142-// Spec pose-review-readiness-before-seal R4: the current brief asks the\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-143-// reviewer to check its draft before finishing; /7 never did.\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-144-func TestTheCurrentBriefAsksForTheDraftCheck(t *testing.T) {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go:145:\tconst step = \"pipe it to `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\" review draft-check --draft -`\"\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-146-\tcurrent := renderReviewBriefBody(fixedBriefBundle(), ReviewBriefKindReview, reviewBriefTemplates[ReviewBriefKindReview], \"\", nil, &ReviewRound{Number: 1}, true, nil)\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-147-\tif !strings.Contains(current, step) {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-148-\t\tt.Fatal(\"the current review brief does not ask for the draft check\")\n--\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-154-}\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-155-\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-156-// Spec pose-assess-leaves-sealed-checkouts-alone R5: the current brief has\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go:157:// the planned `pose` commands run by the dispatching engine; /8 never did.\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-158-func TestTheCurrentBriefRunsPlannedToolsWithTheDispatchingEngine(t *testing.T) {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go:159:\tconst step = \"Run each `pose` command below as `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\"`\"\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-160-\tcurrent := renderReviewBriefBody(fixedBriefBundle(), ReviewBriefKindReview, reviewBriefTemplates[ReviewBriefKindReview], \"\", nil, &ReviewRound{Number: 1}, true, nil)\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go:161:\tif !strings.Contains(current, step) || !strings.Contains(current, \"pipe it to `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\" review draft-check --draft -`\") {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go:162:\t\tt.Fatal(\"the current review brief does not run planned tools and the draft check with the dispatching engine\")\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-163-\t}\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-164-\tearlier := renderReviewBriefBody(fixedBriefBundle(), ReviewBriefKindReview, reviewBriefTemplateDraftCheck, \"\", nil, &ReviewRound{Number: 1}, true, nil)\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go-165-\tif strings.Contains(earlier, step) {\n--\nPOSE.md-390-- Action destination scoping (spec `pose-action-target-and-node-scoping`) — origin is provenance, targets select relevance, and effects select restrictions. An empty effect scope still means the whole source artifact; a roadmap or milestone expands its current members. Explicit scopes replace that default, so a request from alpha can restrict beta without blocking alpha. A named whole artifact is a whole restriction, not partial merely because it is qualified. Pending requests remain visible when their origin becomes terminal. Unknown membership or unreadable material is reported as limited coverage and never expands into every spec. CLI accepts `--effect-json '{\"phase\":\"start\",\"mode\":\"block\",\"scope\":[\"xref:project/spec:beta\"]}'`; MCP `effects` accepts the same object alongside legacy `\"start:block\"` strings. Targets and subjects resolve by project/artifact/kind/ID, and a subject is re-read in its recorded artifact. Under adopted agency readiness, new approvals and acceptances require an explicit `--subject` / `subject`; legacy unbound requests retain their journal and report that answers have no content-change detection. Policy-off creation and transition defaults remain unchanged. This compatibility tightening requires release review; no policy adoption happens here.\nPOSE.md-391-- Signed answers (spec `pose-signed-action-answers`) — a principal proves an answer with an SSH key the project registered, with no external service. `pose identity add [<principal>] --key <file.pub> [--role <role>] --apply` records the key under `keys` in `.pose/policy/actions.json` (only `ssh-ed25519` and `sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com`; a key belongs to one principal; a private key file is refused); without a principal it suggests `human:<name>` from the git identity, which is a name, not a proof. `pose identity list [--json]` shows principals, roles and fingerprints; `pose identity remove <principal> [--fingerprint <fp>] --apply` stops future answers by a key without touching roles or answers already recorded. `pose action resolve … --sign <private key>` signs with the user's own `ssh-keygen -Y sign -n pose-action-answer` (POSE never reads the key); `--signature <file>` attaches a signature made elsewhere over the exact bytes `pose action statement <act-id> --actor <p> --answer <a> --idempotency-key <k>` prints, which bind project, request id and digest, actor, answer and idempotency key. POSE verifies the SSHSIG natively, records statement, signature and key on the answer, and `pose action show` verifies it again from the journal. A signed answer is `verified` under any assurance; under `identity_assurance: verified` an answer needs a registered key's signature or a trusted issuer's claim — two emitters of identity proof, Harne8 being one issuer. Presence follows the explicit channel contract below. `pose doctor` (`actions.keys`) warns when a human role holder under verified assurance can prove no answer, or signs only with keys that cannot prove presence (an error under `require_presence`).\nPOSE.md-392-- Policy scope validation (spec `pose-policy-scope-validation`) — `identity_assurance` and `reviewer_independence` accept only exact `spec`, `milestone` and `roadmap` keys in every supported review-policy schema, including disabled configuration. Empty keys, typos, whitespace, case variants and future kinds are refused with the field, quoted key and supported kinds; no key is normalized and no operator file is repaired automatically. Unknown top-level metadata remains permitted. This stricter refusal is breaking and requires major-release classification, without automatic migration or adoption. Current preparation, `state --governance` and MCP `pose_project_state` report invalid policy explicitly; no partial projection advertises protection. A protected Git baseline with an invalid selector stops new preparation instead of falling back to an unprotected warning. Global governance entries describe instance configuration and name the protected kinds; `--scope`/`governance_scope` adds historical bundle context, not per-scope capability evaluation. Malformed context selectors are refused. Historical sealed records and their stamped gates remain readable and unchanged; inability to evaluate current policy is not a retrospective invalidation of those records.\nPOSE.md:393:- Human presence (spec `pose-proof-presence-channel-parity`) — preserve roles and keys when explicitly migrating `.pose/policy/actions.json` to `schema_version: 2`. Presence defaults to disabled; when `require_presence: true`, choose `presence_scope: \"universal\"` or `\"sshsig\"`. Universal requires a registered SK signature with an authenticated user-present assertion for every human answer, including issuer-backed answers. SSHSIG scope requires it when an SSH signature is supplied; issuer-only answers are explicitly outside that scope and establish no presence. An issuer claim may accompany an SK signature, but every supplied proof must verify. Identity grants, Portal authentication and user-verification alone are not presence. Agents do not acquire human-presence assurance. Enabled schema-1 policy requires migration before new human answers; unknown scopes are refused and older engines reject schema 2. This is a breaking change, requiring major-release classification, without automatic policy adoption. Previews disclose current policy as not-evaluated and never request a touch. Historical reads reverify recorded proof, disclose the key's assertion separately from identity, and leave historical presence requirements unrecorded; they never impose today's scope retrospectively. A verified assertion does not attest hardware provenance or who touched the key.\nPOSE.md-394-- Guided setup (spec `pose-setup-command`) — `pose setup` shows, in one place, the project identity, principals and keys (with the principal the git identity suggests, which is a name and not a proof), the pre-commit gate, capabilities in force, capabilities new since the last configuration review, what needs setup, whether the configuration is committed, and one next step with its command. At a terminal it offers each open step — the hook, registering a key with the `maintainer` role, each new capability (adopt, decline or defer with a reason) — and performs it only after an explicit yes; with `--json`, `--no-input` or no terminal it changes nothing. New means off, undecided and introduced after `reviewed_version` in `.pose/policy/adoption-decisions.json` (every off, undecided capability when none is recorded); a fresh install records its engine version, deciding the last new capability advances it, and a deferral is asked again under a newer engine. `pose install` ends with `pose setup` as the next step, `pose update` names the pending decisions instead of \"Nothing to do\", and `pose doctor` reports them as a `next` step (`setup.capabilities`).\nPOSE.md-395-- Onboarding spec (spec `pose-onboarding-spec`) — a new instance starts with a governed first spec, `.pose/specs/<date>-pose-onboarding.md` (draft, in the install's locale): adopting POSE is the project's first piece of work, with requirements for the declared identity, a maintainer able to prove answers, the commit gate, the reviewed capability defaults and the committed instance, tasks that point to `pose setup`, a decision recording the defaults, and checks (`pose doctor`, `pose check --strict`). It passes lint, the strict check and the Definition of Ready as written. `pose setup` names `pose start spec:pose-onboarding` first and, once every other step is done, `pose close spec:pose-onboarding`. Only an install that creates the instance writes it; `pose update` and a reinstall never create or overwrite it.\nPOSE.md-396-- Configuration review (spec `pose-update-configuration-review`) — when `pose update` finds capabilities this project has not decided for the engine it runs, it writes `.pose/specs/<date>-pose-configuration-review-<version>.md` (draft, one requirement per capability with its effect, version and recommendation) and opens one decision request per capability (`adopt`, `decline`, `defer`; `adopt` recommended for a capability new instances get), addressed to the `maintainer` role and restricting only the review spec's closeout. Nothing is adopted by the update. The maintainer answers with `pose action resolve` (signed when the policy asks, or over MCP with `pose_action_resolve`) or at a `pose setup` prompt; `pose adopt --request <act-id> [--apply]` applies an answered request — adopt turns the capability on dated that day, decline and defer record the decision with the answer's reason and the request id — and refuses an open, cancelled or foreign request. A second update for the same version asks nothing again; `pose setup` lists open review requests with the resolve command and answered ones not yet applied.\n--\nPOSE.md-424-- `doctor [--fix]` — environment, dependency, native runtime, and POSE instance health diagnostics, plus governance that would otherwise fail only downstream: `review.evidence-vocabulary` (a selected profile demands a class no check may emit), `validate.class-producers` (a criterion accepts only classes no registered check produces), `validate.evidence-class-coverage` (checks with no `evidenceClass`), `review.profile-schema`, `review.contract-adoption`, `review.scope-change-set`, and `<policy>.policy-keys` for nine policies (keys the engine does not read, i.e. a misspelled setting that silently does nothing).\nPOSE.md-425-- `knowledge-housekeeping` / `reports-housekeeping` / `events-housekeeping` — idempotent maintenance (list/archive/purge). Mutations require `--apply`. Reports housekeeping **never touches `history/`**: the JSONL is the source of truth for `recurrence-check` and `report`'s temporal comparisons. Defaults: stale = 120d, archive purge = 365d.\nPOSE.md-426-- `amend` — append-only spec amendment history (`amendments.jsonl` beside a folder spec's `spec.md`, or `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.amendments.jsonl` beside a flat dated spec; the spec is resolved through the store, so both layouts amend alike). `--baseline` snapshots every R-ID hash; `--ids R2 --change added|withdrawn|semantic|editorial --rationale <text> --author @alias [--reviewer @alias]` acknowledges a material change; `--list` renders history and pending acknowledgments. On `done` specs with a history, `lint-spec` rejects any requirement whose current text is not acknowledged by an event — specs cannot be silently rewritten after evidence. `--nodes [--json]` prints the versioned contract-node projection: requirements, assumptions and decisions with namespace, content hash, state and relations. With `contract_nodes_version: 1` adopted in the review policy, `--baseline` writes a schema-2 R/A/D snapshot, `--ids` accepts A/D, `--change transition` acknowledges an allowed state change, `editorial` is refused when a node changed state or relations, and the gate also rejects an active decision resting on an invalidated or withdrawn assumption. Without the capability the gate stays requirement-only and a schema-2 event is refused; schema-1 logs keep their meaning.\nPOSE.md:427:- `start` — atomic start of a draft spec, journaled in a per-spec start record. Without flags it is a read-only, digest-bound preview: readiness and dependencies, the declared review obligations (no future structural delta needed) and the R/A/D baseline. `--apply --digest <sha256>` records that baseline and moves the spec from `draft` to `in-progress` under a per-spec lock with compare-and-swap; it needs `atomic_start_version: 1` in the review policy, repeats idempotently, resumes an interrupted run and refuses another plan for a started spec. `--cancel` undoes a start that never moved the spec. The per-spec lock records its owner (host, digests of the machine and boot IDs, PID namespace, PID and process start); a lock another call holds is reported, never broken by age, as `owner-active`, `owner-unknown` or `owner-dead` (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`). Dead is proven only on the same Linux machine — another boot, or a PID that is not running or now belongs to another process — and `--recover-lock <owner digest>`, with the digest `--status` shows, removes that lock only if its bytes and its dead owner are unchanged, leaving the start record to resume; an unknown owner (another host, an older engine's empty lock, another platform) is checked and removed by a person. The same lock is the spec's lifecycle lock: close and transfer take it too, so no two of them interleave on one spec (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`). `--status` classifies each node as `recorded-before-managed-execution`, `introduced-during-execution` or `legacy-unbaselined`, recomputes obligations and, under the capability, asks for reconciliation when status or the recorded baseline was edited by hand. `atomic_start_adopted_at: YYYY-MM-DD` (spec `pose-atomic-start-adoption-cutoff`) limits the capability to specs created on or after that date: an older spec in progress without a start is reported as `legacy-unbaselined` with a note and blocks nothing; without the date every such spec needs reconciliation. A start records observable precedence, not when anything was decided; MCP exposes the read-only view as `pose_start_status`.\nPOSE.md-428-- `knowledge-usage` — projects `knowledge:<slug>` citations from specs per artifact (owner, expiry, citing specs). Usage signals inform the owner's review; TTL is never extended automatically. Dangling `knowledge:` refs fail `knowledge-check`.\nPOSE.md-429-- `knowledge-suggest <query>` / `semantic-suggest <query>` / `suggest-feedback` / `portfolio-projection` / `reconcile-evidence` — deterministic lexical ranking and advisory projections for knowledge, feedback clustering, multi-roadmap portfolio milestones, and delivery validation evidence reconciliation. Suggestions never gate or auto-apply without confirmation.\nPOSE.md-430-- `hooks` — links the native binary into `.git/hooks/`; invocation name selects `check --tolerant` for `pre-commit` and `index` for `post-merge`. `install --force` keeps a backup of pre-existing hooks. An instance that runs POSE through its own revision-bound CLI declares a runner in a `hooks.json` file of `.pose/policy/` (`{\"schema_version\":1,\"runner\":\"<project-relative executable>\"}`): the hook then runs the runner with the hook's name and returns its exit code, and `hooks status` names it; a runner outside the project, not executable or under another schema is refused and nothing runs.\nPOSE.md-431-- `version` — displays compiled Go binary version, commit SHA, and compares the repository schema version with engine requirements.\nPOSE.md-432-- `context` — resolves a selected logical project and optional local or `xref:`-qualified task through the shared artifact resolver. The JSON result includes canonical authority, project/artifact revisions, resolution state, supported contracts and a `context_revision`; it never includes filesystem roots. Use this token to detect a changed checkout or revision before a cross-project write.\nPOSE.md:433:- Cross-project CLI writes require the target in `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` and the `context_revision` from a fresh `pose context` call. Use `new-spec-qualified` for qualified creation so older engines reject the command itself. A project found only by `HARNE8_PROJECTS_DIR` scan can be read but does not grant write access. Existing canonical specs are reused; redirects and incomplete transfers block shadow creation.\nPOSE.md-434-- `install <dir> [--locale tag] [--skip-mcp] [--force] [--no-backup] [--allow-non-git]` — installs the embedded POSE runtime, rules, workflows, templates, and documentation into a target project directory without cloning, stamps `adopted_at` in the changelog policy with the day the instance received it, and runs the strict gate.\nPOSE.md-435-- `import <spec-kit|openspec> <path> [--dry-run]` — imports foreign specification trees into canonical POSE specifications with standard frontmatter and section structure.\nPOSE.md-436-- `serve-mcp` — starts the POSE Model Context Protocol server over stdio (`--stdio`) or HTTP for native AI agent integration. The stdio server exits on SIGTERM; it resolves the `pose` CLI it runs once, at start, so a server started before `pose update` keeps working with the updated binary.\n--\nPOSE.md-448-CLI writes require the target in `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` and the current\nPOSE.md-449-`context_revision` through `--expect-context`; discovering a project through\nPOSE.md-450-`HARNE8_PROJECTS_DIR` allows reading but does not grant write authorization.\nPOSE.md:451:For qualified creation, use `new-spec-qualified`: older engines refuse the\nPOSE.md-452-command before interpreting new options as ignored arguments.\nPOSE.md-453-\nPOSE.md-454-- Treat `.mcp.json`, the connected server process and the selected project as\n--\nPOSE.md-1073-\nPOSE.md-1074-**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/9` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/9` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\nPOSE.md-1075-\nPOSE.md:1076:**Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\nPOSE.md-1077-\nPOSE.md-1078-**Delegated-review requests.** `pose review request <scope|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <name,...>] [--note-file <path>] [--apply]` opens an action request of kind `delegated-review` that binds the project, the worktree, the scope, the sealed bundle, the brief, the notes, the policies, the accepted agents and the destination of the conclusion (an attestation, an adjudication record or smoke evidence). No actor answers it: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <key> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <file>] [--apply]`, or the MCP tool `pose_action_fulfil`, submits a completed run, and the engine records the fulfilment only when the run is of the request's brief and bundle, of an accepted agent, completed with its containment, its signed proof verifies, its attempt ledger is intact and the engine recorded its conclusion at the request's destination; the fulfilment is verified again on every read. `pose delegate review <scope> --request <act-id>` runs the request's brief locally and submits the run once concluded. A rejecting run fulfils the request without approving anything; a superseded bundle or changed policy invalidates it, even once fulfilled, and it is asked again with `--supersedes <act-id>`, which retires it under the supersession authority rules (`--authority <json>`, `--role <role>`, `--requested-at` from the preview), or, when the plan now reserves the review for a person, makes the person's escalation its successor; a retry with the same key and run is a no-op. A person is asked by exception, as a decision request to the maintainer role: a plan that reserves the review for a person, a spent attempt budget, reviewers in conflict, and a `business_decision` a reviewer raised in its draft. A run's proof verifies in any checkout of the project: the worktree it ran in is recorded, not required of the reader, so a CI job or the Conductor fulfils the request with the same envelope.\nPOSE.md-1079-\n--\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go-387-\t\tline(\"## Tools the plan requires\")\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go-388-\t\tline(\"\")\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go-389-\t\tif template == reviewBriefTemplateEngineTools {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:390:\t\t\tline(\"Run each `pose` command below as `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\"` followed by the same arguments: it is the engine that sealed and dispatched this run. The `pose` your shell resolves may be an older installed engine that ignores flags such as `--no-persist` and writes into this sealed checkout, which fails the run.\")\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go-391-\t\t\tline(\"\")\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go-392-\t\t}\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go-393-\t\ttools := append([]ReviewPlanTool(nil), plan.Tools...)\n--\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go-501-\t\t\t\tline(\"%d. %s\", next+2, briefRulesText(template, bundle))\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go-502-\t\t\t}\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go-503-\t\t\tif reviewBriefFromDraftCheck(template) {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:504:\t\t\t\tline(\"%d. Before you finish, check your final message: pipe it to `\\\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\\\" review draft-check --draft -`, the engine that dispatched this run (it reads this run's bundle and round, writes nothing and works in a read-only sandbox) and fix every reason it reports. A draft it refuses is refused at conclusion, and the round is spent.\", next+3)\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go-505-\t\t\t}\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go-506-\t\t}\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go-507-\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
